EXAMPLES=[{'num':'17','title':'Central and Inscribed Angles','question':'For x²+y² = 9 and B(2,√5), C(2,−√5), show the central angle of minor arc BC is twice the inscribed angle at A(−3,0) on the major arc.','steps':['The three points lie on the radius-3 circle: B and C have squared norms 4+5 = 9, and A has squared norm 9.','At A, the rays to B and C are (5,√5) and (5,−√5). Their dot product is 25−5 = 20, and cross magnitude is 10√5.','Thus θ = atan2(10√5,20) = atan(√5/2) ≈ 48.189685°.','At O, the rays OB and OC have dot product 4−5 = −1 and cross magnitude 4√5.','The minor central angle is atan2(4√5,−1) ≈ 96.379370°, which is obtuse because the dot product is negative.','Half the central angle has tangent √5/2: the symmetric rays each form angle atan(√5/2) with the positive x-axis. Therefore ∠BOC = 2θ exactly.','The source’s tangent identity gives tan(2θ) = −4√5; its negative value requires the obtuse branch, which the ray calculation confirms.','Swapping B and C as in the source figure changes their order but leaves the unsigned central and inscribed angles unchanged.'],'answer':'∠BAC ≈ 48.189685° and ∠BOC ≈ 96.379370° = 2∠BAC.','progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'page':235,'pageEnd':236,'jpg':'122_page_1.jpg','verification':'Complete source example, swapped figure labels and continuation visually checked'}],'sourceQuestionBlocks':[central_angles(True)]}]
